@@ -55,6 +55,19 @@ assumed — the tool name and observed behaviour are given so the claim is check
 | `SEC-HARDEN-GAP-001` | **GAP, narrowed 2026-08-22 — the library landed; the drafter did not.** `/runtime-harden`, the second Zone-3 drafter, is specified in three design docs and still exists nowhere: measured 2026-08-11, no file matches `*harden*` on any host, and re-checked 2026-08-22, still none. What changed is the other half of the sentence this row used to carry. The deployed-runtime library is no longer absent — `governance/runtime_dispatcher.py` and `Security-kit/runtime_screen.py` ship and are tested (the design-era path `Security-kit/runtime/` was never created; the modules live beside their build-time counterparts instead). So the gap is now exactly one thing: **no command generates the per-project wiring**, and a reader has to follow `SECURITY.md` S1.6 by hand. Nothing is falsely claimed — the row exists so the absence is stated rather than assumed | design only — no file. The library it was to configure now exists | none | Create the file and add it to `ZONE3_DRAFTERS` in the same commit; `SEC-RUNTIME-GAP-001` tracks whether an application actually routes through the library |
 | `SEC-KIRO-GAP-001` | **GAP** — the Kiro host's Zone-3 drafter is checked by **text presence only**. Its five guardrails now match I5 (fixed 2026-08-15, from a measured 2/5), but no test drives a Kiro session, and `inclusion: auto` is asserted by the presence of a frontmatter key — nothing verifies that Kiro loaded the file, or that a Kiro-hosted `/security-tailor` run obeyed a word of it. Claude-host-only verification is a scoping decision, not a claim the mirror is safe | `kiro/steering/security-tailor.md`; `kiro/steering/active-controls.md` | `python3 tests/test_mechanisms.py` covers guardrail *text*, nothing covers host *behaviour* | Fix: a Kiro-host integration proof, or accept as residual and say so at sign-off |
 
+## Runtime-mvp profile (deployed, opt-in — see SEC-RUNTIME-GAP-001)
+
+These controls enforce in the deployed `runtime-mvp` profile, where no IDE hook events exist. Each is MECHANICAL — an in-process path enforces it and a test proves it — and each is reached only if the application routes through the runtime host. That routing is the umbrella gap `SEC-RUNTIME-GAP-001`, which stays open until a deployment proves it. No production verdict is implied by these rows.
+
+| Control ID | Objective and boundary | Implementation location | Verification | Review evidence |
+|---|---|---|---|---|
+| `SEC-INGRESS-001` | **MECHANICAL** — Binding ①/④ for the deployed profile: rule hit or any semantic non-`data` withholds to REQUIRE_REVIEW; `unresolved`+`data` is the only ALLOW. Detection is patterns + one encoder, so a confident classifier miss admits content (R-1) — the action gate is then the authority. Reached only if the application routes through the host (SEC-RUNTIME-GAP-001). | `Security-kit/runtime/ingress.py` `evaluate_ingress` | `python3 tests/runtime/test_ingress.py` | Runtime-mvp review; `Context/runtime-security-profile.md` |
+| `SEC-SESSION-001` | **MECHANICAL** — The first control at position ⑤ (the sequence): per-tool and total session ceilings, concurrency-safe. Composes around the action gate; owns no gate predicate. Bounds one session only — cross-session memory is a disabled capability. Opt-in routing per SEC-RUNTIME-GAP-001. | `Security-kit/runtime/session.py` `reserve` | `python3 tests/runtime/test_guarded.py` | Runtime-mvp review; `Context/runtime-security-profile.md` |
+| `SEC-CONTENT-RECEIPT-001` | **MECHANICAL** — Releases exactly one content digest, once, under the policy in force at review. Type/key/context separated from action approval — a content receipt cannot authorize an action (TypeError). Authority is only as sound as the reviewer; the receipt records who and when, not that they looked. | `Security-kit/runtime/review.py` `verify_content` | `python3 tests/runtime/test_content_receipts.py` | Runtime-mvp review; `Context/runtime-security-profile.md` |
+| `SEC-ACTION-RECEIPT-001` | **MECHANICAL** — Un-pauses a REQUIRE_APPROVAL action; never converts DENY — the inner gates still run after approval (test-pinned). Binds the exact action digest, expires, single-use. Same reviewer-attestation residual as SEC-CONTENT-RECEIPT-001. | `Security-kit/runtime/review.py` `verify_action` | `python3 tests/runtime/test_action_receipts.py` | Runtime-mvp review; `Context/runtime-security-profile.md` |
+| `SEC-STARTUP-001` | **MECHANICAL** — An unsupported capability (memory, delegation, streaming), a registry/policy mismatch, a missing key, an unbounded audit, a writable production control root or a drifted classifier lock is a StartupError, not a warning. Checks configuration, not the OS confinement around it. | `Security-kit/runtime/startup.py` `validate_startup` | `python3 tests/runtime/test_startup.py` | Runtime-mvp review; `Context/runtime-security-profile.md` |
+| `SEC-OUTPUT-001` | **MECHANICAL** — Buffers the whole final response, redacts credential shapes (owner: secret_scan._PATTERNS, not copied here), known secret values and protected fragments, then releases — no byte leaves before screening. Redaction is pattern-and-value based; an unlisted secret shape passes. | `Security-kit/runtime/output.py` `screen_output` | `python3 tests/runtime/test_output.py` | Runtime-mvp review; `Context/runtime-security-profile.md` |
+
